{"chapter": 5, "sha256": "5ee87c733d2c04c1326e4c58571567097d2cdca086ea7ea878a5280c3eefe5fb", "voice": {"voice_id": "ppLqTilh7rH7fbUVlXsf", "name": "David - Documentary", "model_id": "eleven_v3", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.8, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}}, "source": "docs/script.md", "files": {"narration": {"file": "ch5.mp3", "text": "In 1989 Robert Bauval argued that Giza's three pyramids copy Orion's belt: two nearly equal, the third smaller and off the line, like Mintaka. A 2026 study ranks the match in the top quarter of one per cent of almost 700,000 star triplets, but calls it \"real but narrow.\"\n\nBauval and Hancock read the plan as a clock. The Earth's wobble raises and lowers the belt over 26,000 years, and about 10,500 BCE it hits the bottom of its swing: the First Time, they say. But Seked's engine puts the belt thirteen degrees off the pyramids' line then, and the swing is too flat to name a century.\n\nInside the Great Pyramid, a shaft from the King's Chamber climbs south at 45 degrees. About 2450 BCE Alnitak, the belt's easternmost star, crossed the meridian within a fifth of a degree of that line. So the stone holds two dates, they say: its building and its plan. The shafts, say astronomers, date only the building, in Khufu's century.", "seconds": 72.19, "alignment": {"characters": ["I", "n", " ", "1", "9", "8", "9", " ", "R", "o", "b", "e", "r", "t", " ", "B", "a", "u", "v", "a", "l", " ", "a", "r", "g", "u", "e", "d", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "G", "i", "z", "a", "'", "s", " ", "t", "h", "r", "e", "e", " ", "p", "y", "r", "a", "m", "i", "d", "s", " ", "c", "o", "p", "y", " ", "O", "r", "i", "o", "n", "'", "s", " ", "b", "e", "l", "t", ":", " ", "t", "w", "o", " ", "n", "e", "a", "r", "l", "y", " ", "e", "q", "u", "a", "l", ",", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "t", "h", "i", "r", "d", " ", "s", "m", "a", "l", "l", "e", "r", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "o", "f", "f", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "l", "i", "n", "e", "